#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151896 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78026cd2-f1f0-56cc-a2d2-8d73c40e97df" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Particular Solution For a Cauchy-Euler Equation with Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "62bf44e3-c80e-514d-aa22-221cce8288f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-6633" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$t^2 y'' - 4ty' + 6y = 2t+6, \\quad t > 0$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(t) = At^3 + Bt^2$, find the particular solution $y_p(t)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2t+6$. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = \\alpha t + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = \\alpha, \\qquad y_p'' = 0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2}(0) - 4t(α) + 6(αt + β) &= 2t + 6 \\\\\n(-4α + 6α)t + 6β &= 2t + 6 \\\\\n2αt + 6β &= 2t + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{2α = 2\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients oft) \\\\ 6β = 6\\end{vmatrix}(equating the constants)\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = 1$ and $\\beta =1$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = t + 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151897 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff16b5e7-d25d-58f1-83d3-e8d4a92d7064" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation With Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "5e128ba1-9fb8-5d4b-bd54-4af5bb375cd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6634" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^2 y'' + xy' - y = 3x^2, \\quad x>0$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complimentary function $y_c(x)$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\nx^2y'' + xy' - y = 0\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)x^{λ - 2} + x \\cdot λx^{λ - 1} - x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nλ(λ - 1)x^{λ} + λx^{λ} - x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{λ}[λ(λ - 1) + λ - 1] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ(λ - 1) + λ - 1 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)(λ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}+ c_2 x\n$$\n\nNow, we find the particular solution. The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation $(3x^2)$ contains only an $x^2$ term, so we assume that the particular solution is also of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha x^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =2 \\alpha x, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 2 \\alpha\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha$, we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation and group the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}(2α) + x(2αx) - αx^{2} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\n(2α + 2α - α)x^{2} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\n3αx &= 3x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get $\\alpha = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =x^2\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y_{c}(x) + y_{p}(x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{c_{1}}{x} + c_{2}x + x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151898 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85d71b68-d4d4-53c9-8ff7-135389e85edc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Particular Solution For a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "819a6bed-b738-5bda-b341-e3f5137aac4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6635" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$t^2 y'' +2ty' = t^2 e ^ {t}, \\quad t>0$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(t) = \\dfrac{A}{t} + B$, find the particular solution $y_p(t)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side is not a polynomial, so we need to use another solution technique. Here, we are given the complementary solution\n\n$$\ny_c(t) = \\dfrac{A}{t} + B\n$$\n\nwhich means that we have two fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(t) = \\dfrac{1}{t}, \\qquad y_2(t) = 1\n$$\n\nBecause we have two fundamental solutions, we can use variation of parameters.\n\nTo use variation of parameters, we must first reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\ny'' + P(t) y' +Q(t) y = f(t)\n$$\n\nTo do this, we divide both sides of the given equation by $t^2$, and get\n\n$$\ny'' + \\dfrac{2}{t}y' = e ^ {t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\ny_1(t) = \\dfrac{1}{t}, \\qquad y_2(t) = 1, \\qquad f(t) = e^t\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{t})(1)^{′} - (\\frac{1}{t})^{′}(1) \\\\\n&= 0 + \\frac{1}{t^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(t)$ and $y_2(t)$ are independent.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, a particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(t) &= y_{1}(t)u(t) + y_{2}(t)v(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(t) &= - ∫(f(t)y_{2}(t))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dt = - ∫t^{2}e^{t} = - (t^{2} - 2t + 2)e^{t}, \\\\\nv(t) &= ∫(f(t)y_{1}(t))/(W(y_{1}, y_{2}))dt = ∫te^{t} = (t - 1)e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a particular solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}(t) &= y_{1}(t)u(t) + y_{2}(t)v(t) \\\\\n&= - ((t^{2} - 2t + 2)e^{t})/(t) + (t - 1)e^{t} \\\\\n&= (1 - \\frac{2}{t})e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151899 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35013c7e-161d-57b9-8b70-9180bf4e5f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "e3463872-d79a-5c63-bd78-31e0e206306b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6636" :question/problem "Find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ of the equation\n$x^2y''+xy' + y = \\ln x, \\quad x>0$." :question/worked-solution "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x=e^t$, we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x=e^t$, we note that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = e^t = x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm d t}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac1x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{dt}\\frac{dt}{dx} = \\frac{1}{x}\\frac{dy}{dt}, \\\\\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{dy}{dt}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} + \\frac{1}{x} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{dy}{dt})_(⏟)_(product rule) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\frac{dy}{dt} + \\frac{1}{x} \\cdot \\frac{dt}{dx}\\frac{d}{dt}_(⏟)_(chain rule)(\\frac{dy}{dt}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\frac{dy}{dt} + \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dt^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dt^{2}} - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\frac{dy}{dt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{d^{2}y}{dt^{2}} + y = t\n$$\n\nThis equation is a second-order inhomogeneous ODE equation with constant coefficients and polynomial forcing.\n\nUsing the usual methods for solving second-order ODEs, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A\\cos t+B\\sin t\n$$\n\nSince right-hand side of the inhomogeneous ODE is a polynomial of degree $1$, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1$, i.e.\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=\\alpha t+ \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t)$:\n\n$$\n0+(\\alpha t+\\beta)=t\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{α = 1\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of [math]t[/math]) \\\\ β = 0\\end{vmatrix}(equating the constants)\n$$\n\nThis system has the solution $\\alpha=1$ and $\\beta=0$.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n$y_p(t) = t$.\n\nNow, all that remains is to write the above solution in terms of the original variable $x$. Since $x=e^t$, we have $t = \\ln x$, and therefore\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =\\ln x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff16b5e7-d25d-58f1-83d3-e8d4a92d7064" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation With Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "802521a1-802a-56c7-ae7b-f56eb7fa226d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x^{2} - 3x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150145} {:answer/id #uuid "83d1f87f-168e-57e2-938b-6bdaf9a8a9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x^{2} + x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150146} {:answer/id #uuid "e5deb82f-ccf5-5388-807d-d79595361660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\frac{c_{1}}{x} + c_{2}x + x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150147} {:answer/id #uuid "ff955bf6-62cd-5b76-8058-69d0a516c1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x^{2} + 4x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150148} {:answer/id #uuid "42e9763c-e81f-5b64-bffc-f706d6812a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\frac{c_{1}}{x} + c_{2}x - 2x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83d1f87f-168e-57e2-938b-6bdaf9a8a9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x^{2} + x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150146} :answer-field/id #uuid "26e07f4b-d9ab-50a4-b4b5-08ff02ea40f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150144}] :question/id #uuid "3198200a-8c30-50e5-8dc8-77fc454318d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64871" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^{2}y^{″} - 2xy^{′} + 2y = 2x^{3}, x > 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35013c7e-161d-57b9-8b70-9180bf4e5f9d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ad3d1c-57b5-59b8-b207-93a7943ff425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = x^{2}\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150157} {:answer/id #uuid "8fccb1d0-0cc1-5ced-a71f-d657e3d0a81f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = x\\ln x + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150158} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c6f0ee-5a1c-5f90-aaed-93d60b3041a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = 2\\ln x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150159} {:answer/id #uuid "22795e9c-cc15-50c7-abbb-b70eb6f8a0c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = \\ln x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150160} {:answer/id #uuid "48d4122f-ee94-5560-ba6a-b556d8eb071f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = 2\\ln x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22795e9c-cc15-50c7-abbb-b70eb6f8a0c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = \\ln x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150160} :answer-field/id #uuid "884da4a3-0619-5b9b-abea-9e92d8d9c8f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150156}] :question/id #uuid "2eddeffd-354f-5e9b-a7ba-50ad11a83d60" :question/math-academy-id "q-64878" :question/problem "Find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ of the equation\n$x^{2}\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 3x\\frac{dy}{dx} + 4y = 4\\ln x, x > 0$.\n\n*Hint: Consider using the substitution $x = e^{t}$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85d71b68-d4d4-53c9-8ff7-135389e85edc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Particular Solution For a Cauchy-Euler Equation Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ad261c0-b614-588f-9253-dff47033d7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(t) = (3t + 1)e^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150169} {:answer/id #uuid "b84b74fa-eea4-5add-b73c-3ad47e146571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(t) = (t - 2 + \\frac{2}{t})e^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150170} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca63bd6-e385-5b7e-8d15-c646dcc8fbd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(t) = (t^{2} + t + 3)e^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150171} {:answer/id #uuid "7e93275f-0b7d-5f65-84da-12e26835c7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(t) = (t^{2} - 1)e^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150172} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb92ee5-0b83-5d98-8098-025b1655e5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(t) = (4t + \\frac{1}{t})e^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b84b74fa-eea4-5add-b73c-3ad47e146571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(t) = (t - 2 + \\frac{2}{t})e^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150170} :answer-field/id #uuid "52aa0b58-d896-5236-b45c-a1c47221be6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150168}] :question/id #uuid "a5e3f62b-233d-54f3-a008-6926f1db20f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64891" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$t^{2}y^{″} - 2y = t^{3}e^{t}, t > 0$\nhas the complimentary function $y_{c}(t) = \\frac{A}{t} + Bt^{2}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(t)$ to this equation.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n$∫t^{3}e^{t}dt = (t^{3} - 3t^{2} + 6t - 6)e^{t} + C$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff16b5e7-d25d-58f1-83d3-e8d4a92d7064" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation With Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96adf5b2-97ac-5593-8245-5c27d63d332a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\frac{c_{1}}{x^{2}} + c_{2}x^{2} + 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150181} {:answer/id #uuid "cee8b2c3-1352-5c09-85d9-7354ff269b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\frac{c_{1}}{x^{2}} + c_{2}x^{2} - 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150182} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d372e6-9cb3-5423-9b23-784fc95dae9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x^{2} - 5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150183} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0e6f73-5e24-5d0b-aeb6-22680079c70a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = c_{1}x + c_{2}x^{2} + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150184} {:answer/id #uuid "50adcf56-05f5-58ef-8c3a-0cee22139f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math